# Highly Crystalized Cl‐Doped SnO2 Nanocrystals for Stable Aqueous Dispersion Toward High‐Performance Perovskite Photovoltaics

## abstract
Tin dioxide (SnO2 ) with high conductivity and low photocatalytic activity has been reported as one of the best candidates for highly efficient electron transport layer (ETL) in perovskite solar cell (PSC). The state-of-the-art SnO2 layer is achieved by chemical bath deposition with tunable properties, while the commercial SnO2 nanocrystals (NCs) with low tunability still face the necessity of further improvement. Here, a kind of highly crystallized Cl-doped SnO2 NCs is reported that can form very stable aqueous dispersion with shelf life up to one year without any stabilizer, which can facilitate the fabrication of PSCs with satisfactory performance. Compared to the commercial SnO2 NCs regardless of the extrinsic Cl-doping conditions, the intrinsic Cl-doped SnO2 NCs effectively suppress the energy barrier and reduces the trap state density at the buried interface between perovskite and ETL. Consequently, stable PSCs based on such Cl-doped SnO2 NCs achieve a champion efficiency up to ≈25% for small cell (0.085 cm2 ) and ≈20% for mini-module (12.125 cm2 ), indicating its potential as a promising candidate for ETL in high-performance perovskite photovoltaics.

Highly Crystalized Cl-Doped $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ Nanocrystals for Stable Aqueous Dispersion Toward High-Performance Perovskite Photovoltaics
Tin dioxide $\scriptstyle(\mathsf{S n O}_{2})$ with high conductivity and low photocatalytic activity has been reported as one of the best candidates for highly efficient electron transport layer (ETL) in perovskite solar cell (PSC). The state-of-the-art $\mathsf{s n O}_{2}$ layer is achieved by chemical bath deposition with tunable properties, while the commercial $\mathsf{s n O}_{2}$ nanocrystals (NCs) with low tunability still face the necessity of further improvement. Here, a kind of highly crystallized Cl-doped $\mathsf{s n O}_{2}$ NCs is reported that can form very stable aqueous dispersion with shelf life up to one year without any stabilizer, which can facilitate the fabrication of PSCs with satisfactory performance. Compared to the commercial $\mathsf{s n O}_{2}$ NCs regardless of the extrinsic Cl-doping conditions, the intrinsic Cl-doped $\mathsf{s n O}_{2}$ NCs effectively suppress the energy barrier and reduces the trap state density at the buried interface between perovskite and ETL. Consequently, stable PSCs based on such Cl-doped $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ NCs achieve a champion efficiency up to ${\approx}25\%$ for small cell $(0.085\ c m^{2})$ and $\approx20\%$ for mini-module $(12.125\ c m^{2})$ , indicating its potential as a promising candidate for ETL in high-performance perovskite photovoltaics.
## 1. Introduction
Tin dioxide $(\mathsf{S n O}_{2})$ is a wonderful participant to efficiently transport electrons because of its high native mobility that reaches $240\mathsf{c m}^{2}\ (\mathsf{V}\thinspace\mathsf{s})^{-1}$ .[1] In addition, $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ possesses a bandgap that is wide enough for excellent visible light transmittance, with the valence band maximum at a position that is deep enough, which contributes to the hole-blocking ability of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ in the process of electron transfer to avoid electron–hole recombination.[2] Furthermore, $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is insensitive to ultraviolet light, thus avoiding the instability that results from photocatalytic activity.[3] Based on the above superiorities, $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ has been confirmed as an ideal candidate of electron transport layer (ETL) in perovskite solar cells (PSCs).[2a,4] Until now, record power conversion efficiency (PCE) of PSCs has been realized by using $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as the ETL,[5] which also holds greater potential than other candidates to achieve further breakthrough in cell performance. The commercial suspension of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs from Alfa Aesar (Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs) has been widely applied as it combines the advantages of forming films facilely and realizing high-performance PSCs easily.[1b] Previous report confirmed that such suspension needs potassium hydroxide (KOH) as extra stabilizer to obtain uniform aqueous dispersion.[6] KOH also plays a positive role on improving the charge-carrier extraction capability of Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETLs. But the KOH in suspension leads to strong basicity and severe causticity, which appears to adversely affect the long-term stability of the formed aqueous dispersion.[7] Moreover, the Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETLs still need further modifications to suppress the buried interfacial recombination and to reduce the charge-carrier extraction barrier in PSCs.[8]
Doping Cl into the native $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ to form extrinsic Cl-doped $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ (denoted as $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) NCs is an effective strategy to improve the charge-carrier extraction capability of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETLs, but the overall PSC performance based on such $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs is still unsatisfactory.[9] Besides Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs, $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ synthesized with different strategies including solgel,[10] atomic layer deposition,[11] chemical bath deposition,[5a,12] nanocrystals,[13] etc. have also been applied as ETLs in PSCs. But only few of them can be comparable as Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs considering performance and processibility. Hence, we report a type of facile hydrothermally prepared intrinsic Cl-doped $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs as a superior candidate to Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs to prepare PSCs with higher performance. Such intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs can form very stable aqueous dispersion without the assistance of any additional stabilizer. The ETLs casted by such dispersion could reduce the buried interfacial energy barrier and trap state density in the as-prepared PSCs when compared to the ETLs based on Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs regardless of the extrinsic Cl-doping condition. The improvement on open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ and fill factor (FF) pushed the PSCs to achieve a champion PCE up to ${\approx}25\%$ for small-area cell $(0.085\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ ) and $\approx20\%$ for mini-module $(12.125~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ ). In addition, the as-prepared PSCs revealed great stability in ambient environment and 1-sun illumination.
## 2. Results and Discussion
In this work, the introduced Cl- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs were synthesized by hydrothermal reaction with $\mathrm{\SnCl_{4}}$ as the $\mathrm{sn}$ and Cl precursors, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as the O precursor. The detailed method is described in the experimental section. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images in Figure 1a,b reveal that the $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs are well crystallized with an average size of ${\approx}8~\mathrm{nm}$ . Photograph in Figure 1c shows that such $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs can be stably stored under ambient conditions as solid, and readily form aqueous dispersion that is stable up to one year in the absence of any stabilizer. Figure 1d presents the X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) patterns of Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs and synthetic $\mathsf{C l}{\cdot}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\mathsf{N C s}$ (enlarged XRD pattern of Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs is shown in Figure S1 in the Supporting Information). Significantly enhanced peak intensity for the synthetic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs than Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs confirms that the synthetic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs exhibit significantly higher crystallinity. This inference was further verified by the electron diffraction pattern of the synthetic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{Sn}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ NCs as shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information), in which more diffraction rings were detected compared to that of the Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs from previous report.[1b]
Figure 1e shows the O 1s core-level spectra of Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs and synthetic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs. The peak at ${\approx}530.4~\mathrm{eV}$ can be deconvoluted into two peaks both in the cases of Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs and synthetic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs. With one peak belonging to native metal-oxygen bonds, a unique peak at ${\approx}531.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ was separated in Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs that belongs to hydroxyl ion $(\mathrm{OH^{-}})$ . According to previous report, the signal of $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ in the sample of Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs usually results from KOH, which acts as a stabilizer to disperse the Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs in water.[6] The observation of $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ signal further confirms the existence of KOH in this sample (Figure S3, Supporting Information). It also causes strong alkalinity in the dispersion of Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs with a $\mathsf{p H}$ value of ${\approx}12$ (Figure S4, Supporting Information).[6] Meanwhile, in the sample of synthetic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs, a unique peak at $531.9\mathrm{eV}$ belonging to hydroxyl radical $(\mathrm{-OH})$ was separated. Such intrinsic $-\mathrm{OH}$ allows the synthetic Cl- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs to form neutral and mild aqueous dispersion $\mathrm{(pH\approx7.39)}$ without the addition of any acidic or alkaline stabilizers.
Figure 1f shows the core-level spectra of Cl 2p for the Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs with or without methylamine hydrochloride (MACl) added and the synthetic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs. Cl signal was observed for the synthetic Cl- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs, indicating that such NCs were subject to intrinsic Cl-doping. This result was further confirmed by energydispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) mapping (Figure S5, Supporting Information). The absence of Cl signal for the primary Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs indicates that this type of NCs is not intrinsically doped with Cl. They need an additional source of Cl, i.e., MACl, to realize extrinsic Cl-doping. Compared to intrinsic Cl$\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs, Cl $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ signal appears at lower binding energy for extrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs, suggesting that the extrinsic Cl-doping manner results in many free $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ not bound with Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs. Figure $1\mathrm{g}$ presents the core-level spectra of $\mathrm{Sn}\ 3\mathrm{d}$ for pristine Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , extrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , and intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs. Both of $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ signals shifted to higher binding energy after extrinsically doping $\mathrm{Cl}$ into Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs, indicating the density of oxygen vacancies, one major cause of deep-level defects in $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , was reduced.[14] Hence, it confirms that extrinsic Cldoping manner favors to reduce the deep-level defect density of Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs.[2b,15] A further trend towards higher binding energy for these two peaks was found in the intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs, demonstrating that intrinsic Cl-doping manner is superior to the extrinsic one in restraining the deep-level defect in $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs.
To clarify the influence of different Cl-doping manners on the electron extraction of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs, energy-level structure of each case was measured by combining tauc plots (Figure 2a) and ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) (Figure 2b). According to quantum size effect,[16] the narrower bandgap of intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs is due to the larger NC size compared to Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs.[1b] As the scheme shown in Figure 2c, extrinsic Cl-doping manner lowered the conduction band minimum (CBM) of Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs from $-3.67$ to $-3.75\mathrm{eV}_{\mathrm{i}}$ , thus reducing the energy barrier between Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs and $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite from 0.14 to $0.06\mathrm{eV}.$ Such energy barrier was completely removed after using the intrinsic ${\mathrm{Cl}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{SnO}}_{2}$ NCs as the replacement, confirming the superior electron extraction capability of the intrinsic Cl- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs than Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs regardless of the extrinsic Cl-doping condition. To further verify the excellent electron extraction of the intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs, steady-state and time-resolved photoluminescence (PL/TRPL) spectra were performed for the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite films deposited on the different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETLs. As shown in Figure 2d,e, both the PL intensity and lifetime (parameters included in Table S1 in the Supporting Information) were reduced after extrinsically doping Cl into Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs, further decrease on both parameters was observed after using intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{Sn}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ NCs to replace Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs. Strictly speaking, the variation on PL intensity and PL lifetime can be ascribed to both the electron extraction capability of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and the nonradiative recombination caused by trap states. In order to accurately evaluate the capability of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC layer to extract electrons from $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite, the density of trap states $(n_{\mathrm{e}})$ was evaluated according to the trap-filled limit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ originated from the current $(I)$ – voltage (V) curves (Figure 2f) of the electron-only devices (Figure S6, Supporting Information) that were tested in dark. The incorporation of intrinsic ${\mathrm{Cl}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{SnO}}_{2}$ NCs pushed the device to produce a lower $n_{\mathrm{e}}$ than the ones based on the Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs. Such lower $n_{\mathrm{e}}$ results from the intrinsic Cl, which can not only passivate the O vacancies on the upper surface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCbased ETLs, but also form an atomically coherent interlayer of crystalline $\mathrm{FASnCl}_{x}$ at the interface between perovskite and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ to suppress the defects on the bottom surface of perovskite photoactive layer.[17] Hence, these results mean that superior electron extraction was realized by intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{Sn}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ NCs compared to Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs even with extrinsic Cl-doping treatment.
The requirement of preparing $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ PSCs with high performance encouraged us to deposit $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs as ETLs on the FTO substrate that is modified by compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{c}-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)}$ ) using atomic layer deposition (ALD).[18] Comparison on the photovoltaic performance of PSCs with $\mathsf{c}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ modification is shown in Figure S7 in the Supporting Information. For the surface morphology of the Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETLs with or without extrinsic Cl-doping, no significant change was observed from the top-view scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (Figure 3a,b), while the change on surface roughness from 34.6 to $33.5~\mathrm{nm}$ was observed from the height images of atomic force microscopy (AFM) as shown in Figure 3d,e. Meanwhile, the intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs formed a ETL with obviously different surface morphology (Figure 3c), whereas the surface roughness with a value of $33.2\mathrm{nm}$ is similar to the extrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETL (Figure 3f). In addition to the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETLs alone, the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite films that deposited on top were also explored. No obvious difference was observed from the top-view SEM images (Figure S8a–c, Supporting Information), indicating that the substrates composed of different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs in this work have little effect on the crystallization kinetics of the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite film. However, the patterns of the lateral PL distribution of the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite films are very different as shown in Figure 3g–i. The case that deposited on the intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETL displayed a more significant PL quenching than the ones deposited on the Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCbased ETLs regardless of extrinsically doping Cl or not. The mapping results correspond to the profiles in the steady-state PL spectra in Figure S9 (Supporting Information), all suggesting that on the $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{c}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ substrate, intrinsic Cl- $S\mathrm{nO}_{2}$ NC-based ETL is still superior to pristine or extrinsically Cl-doped Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETLs in extracting electrons from $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite film.
Figure 4a exhibits the cross-sectional SEM image of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ PSC, which owns a configuration of ${\mathrm{FTO}}/{\mathrm{c}}.$ $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/S n O_{2}/F A P b I_{3}}$ /Spiro-OMeTAD/Au. Figure $\boldsymbol{4}\mathrm{b}$ presents the structure illustration and photograph of the as-prepared $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite mini-modules. Here, we define the perovskite solar cells based on Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs with or without extrinsic Cl-doping and intrinsic Cl- $\mathrm{\SnO}_{2}$ NCs as pristine, control and target devices, respectively. Figure $4\mathsf{c}$ shows the forward-scan (FS) and reversescan (RS) current density $(J)-V$ curves of the PSCs with a device area of $0.085~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , which were measured under 1-sun illumination (AM 1.5G). The corresponding device parameters are listed in Table 1. Pristine device achieved an optimal PCE of $23.08\%$ with the values of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ , short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ and FF as $1.099\mathrm{V},25.51\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ and $82.34\%$ respectively. In comparison, optimal PCE of the control device increased to $23.76\%$ , which is mainly attributed to the respective enhancement of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF to $1.122\mathrm{~V~}$ and $82.86\%$ . Such increments demonstrated that extrinsic Cl-doping is indeed an effective strategy to improve the performance of Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC ETLs, thus enhancing the efficiency of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ PSCs. Moreover, target device increased the PCE to $24.98\%$ , mainly due to the further enhancement of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF to $1.151\mathrm{~V~}$ and $84.47\%$ , respectively. It is therefore concluded that intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETL is superior to the Alfa ones with or without extrinsic Cl-doping in improving the PCE of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ PSCs. Moreover, we used hysteresis index (HI) to quantify the $J{-}V$ hysteresis.[19] The detailed calculation method can be found in the Supporting Information. The lowest HI was obtained for the target device (Table 1), indicating intrinsic Cl$\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs are beneficial to reduce the hysteresis of perovskite solar cells. Figure 4d presents the external quantum efficiencies (EQEs) of our prepared PSCs. The integrated $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values obtained from EQEs for pristine, control and target devices are 25.47, 25.51, and $25.64~\mathrm{mA~cm^{-2}}$ , respectively, which are well consistent with the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values measured under illumination. To further demonstrate the advantages of using intrinsic Cl- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETL in $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ PSCs, PCE distributions of 16 devices for each case were explored with the results shown in Figure 4e. Pristine devices possess a PCE distribution of $22.19\pm1.01\%$ . While the control devices improve the average PCE to $23.09\pm0.72\%$ , the target devices present the highest PCE average of $24.41\pm0.57\%$ . Figure S10 (Supporting Information) is the stabilized power output (SPO) of the devices tested at maximum power point. The same trend was observed that the target device owns the maximum SPO value of $24.22\%$ , higher than the $23.06\%$ of the control device or the pristine device $(22.29\%)$ . It is worth noting that the KOH originally existing in the Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC aqueous dispersion makes great contribution to the realized performance of the Alfa- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ PSCs (Figure S11, Supporting Information), because potassium cations can passivate the interfacial defects between perovskite and Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ to reduce interfacial nonradiative recombination.[6] Nevertheless, trying to add KOH into the synthesized intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{Sn}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ NC deteriorates the long-term stability of the aqueous dispersion (Figure S12, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the performance of the intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ PSCs also deteriorated (Figure S13, Supporting Information), because KOH not only cannot reduce the interfacial defect density between perovskite and intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ (Figure S14, Supporting Information), but also prevents the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs from extracting carriers from the perovskite photoactive layers (Figure S15, Supporting Information).
Device stability under varied external stresses was then studied and compared. As shown in Figure $\textstyle4\mathrm{f},$ all the devices were stored at the relative humidity (RH) of $30\mathrm{-}40\%$ in dark without any encapsulation for about $2000{\mathrm{h}}$ . Pristine device retained $88.3\%$ of its initial PCE. Control and target devices exhibited better stabilities with $92.5\%$ and $95.1\%$ of the initial PCE maintained, respectively. The test on device stability was also conducted under 1- sun illumination in nitrogen atmosphere. The results shown in Figure ${4\mathrm{g}}$ present the same tendency to the stability test in dark, where the control and target devices exhibited better stabilities than the pristine device. It is suspected that the control device showed better stability than the pristine device due to the suppression of buried interfacial defects that may have acted as the trigger point to deteriorate perovskite structure. The further improved stability of the target device can be attributed to the further reduction of these interfacial defects and the tactical avoidance of strong alkaline corrosion from KOH. We have also demonstrated that such modification to the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL can be extended to facilitate the fabrication of perovskite mini-modules with larger area. Figure 4h presents the FS and RS $J{-}V$ curves of the pristine, control and target mini-modules with an aperture area of $12.125\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ under 1-sun illumination. The active area of the minimodules is $11.398\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . The corresponding device parameters are listed in Table 1. Aperture area-based PCE $\displaystyle(\mathrm{PCE_{ap}})$ of $20.10\%$ was achieved by the mini-module using intrinsic $\mathsf{C l}{\cdot}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ NC-based ETL with an active area-based PCE $(\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{ac}})$ of $21.38\%$ . It is significantly higher than the mini-modules using Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETLs with or without extrinsic Cl-doping, whose $\mathrm{PCE_{ap}}$ values are $16.45\%$ and $17.49\%$ , respectively with the respective $\bar{\mathsf{P C E}}_{\mathrm{ac}}$ value are $17.50\%$ and $18.61\%$ . In addition, intrinsic Cl- $\mathrm{\SnO}_{2}$ NC-based mini-module also exhibited lower hysteresis than the one based on Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs regardless of the extrinsic Cl-doping conditions.
To investigate the reason of the performance enhancement for the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ PSCs based on the intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETL, a series of electrical properties of the prepared devices were studied. Figure 5a presented the electroluminescence (EL) spectra of our PSCs. The injection of current into the PSCs can promote the devices to glow bright red light (photograph inset in Figure 5a). EL intensity emerges an increasing trend for the pristine, control and target devices. As shown in Figure 5b, the maximum output electroluminescence EQE $(\mathrm{EQE_{\mathrm{EL}}})$ holds the same trend as EL intensity between device types, and the target device reveals the highest one of $11.79\%$ . Besides the maximum $\mathrm{EQE}_{\mathrm{EL}}$ value, the $\mathrm{EQE_{EL}}$ at the position where the driving $J$ corresponds to the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ value also deserves our attention. It can be applied to evaluate the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ loss resulted from nonradiative recombination $(\Delta V_{\mathrm{oc}}^{\mathrm{non-rad}})$ by the equation of $\begin{array}{r}{\Delta V_{\mathrm{oc}}^{\mathrm{non-rad}}=-\frac{k T}{q}\mathrm{ln}(\mathrm{EQE}_{\mathrm{EL}})}\end{array}$ , where $k$ , T, and $q$ represents Boltzmann constant, thermodynamic temperature and elementary charge, respectively.[20] According to Figure 5b, the $\mathrm{EQE_{EL}}$ values where the driving $J$ equals to the measured $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ are $3.51\%$ , $6.26\%$ , and $9.27\%$ for pristine, control and target devices, respectively. $\Delta V_{\mathrm{oc}}^{\mathrm{non-rad}}$ can be thus calculated as −145, −130, and $-120\mathrm{mV}$ for the three types of devices. This result indicates that the PSC using intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETL produced a lower $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ -loss that related to nonradiative recombination than the ones based on Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs. The lowest saturated recombination current density $\left(J_{0}\right)$ for the device based on the intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC further confirmed the superior ability of such ETL to restrain $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ -loss related nonradiative recombination than the other two kinds (Figure S16, Supporting Information).[21] Figure 5c shows the variation trend of transient photovoltage with time. In comparison with the pristine and the control device, the highest decay time of $0.73\mathrm{ms}$ was realized by target device. This gives direct evidence on the optimal capacity to restrain nonradiative recombination of the intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETL. Figure 5d shows the trap density of states (tDOS) distribution in devices that were measured by thermal admittance spectroscopy. The lowest density of trap states was demonstrated by the target device, indicating the optimal ability to restrain nonradiative recombination of the intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETL, which may have also been resulted from the best shielding of trap states.
To further evaluate the charge-carrier dynamics in the prepared $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ PSCs, Mott–Schottky plots of the devices were profiled in Figure 5e. The control device reveals a slightly higher built-in potential $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ than the pristine device, while the target device further increases the $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ value to $0.90{\mathrm{V}}.$ Figure S17 in the Supporting Information shows the relationship between effective voltage $(V_{\mathrm{eff}})$ and photocurrent density $(J_{\mathrm{ph}})$ of the prepared devices.[22] At low $V_{\mathrm{eff}}$ region where $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ is not at saturation, control device presents $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ that is higher than the pristine device but lower than the target device. Figure S18 (Supporting Information) shows the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) of the prepared devices that were measured in dark. Decreasing charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{tr}})$ from 3.9 to $2.0\mathrm{k}\Omega$ and $0.8\mathrm{k}\Omega$ , as well as increasing charge recombination resistance $(R_{\mathrm{rec}})$ from 31.8 to $57.6\mathrm{k}\Omega$ and $105.8\mathrm{k}\Omega$ were obtained for the pristine, control and target devices. Figure 5f used the relationship between $-{\frac{\mathrm{d}V}{\mathrm{d}J}}$ and $\frac{\mathit{\Pi}_{\overline{{1}}}}{(J_{s c}-J)}$ to calculate ideal factor $(n)$ .[21] Target device displays a lower $n$ value of 1.26 than the pristine and control devices. All the above results strongly prove that intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based ETL improves the charge-carrier transport capacity inside the cells. In addition, series resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{s}}\right)$ can be calculated based on Figure 5f. A lower value of $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ means that the device has the potential to achieve higher FF.[23] As the control device shows a $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ that is lower than pristine device but higher than target device, it gives the reason on why higher FF was obtained by the device with the ETL using the intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs to replace Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs regardless of the extrinsic Cl-doping.
## 3. Conclusion
In this work, we designed and synthesized $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs with outstanding performance as the electron transport material for perovskite solar cell. They were intrinsically doped with Cl and can form stable aqueous dispersion up to one year without the need of any stabilizer. We found that even if the energy barrier and defect states at the buried interface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based PSCs were significantly reduced by extrinsically doping Cl into the commercial Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs, the device performance still lag behind the PSCs based on our intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs. Through restraining the occurrence of nonradiative recombination to improve the charge-carrier transport capacity, $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ PSCs with the ETLs composed of the intrinsic $\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs showed a champion efficiency of ${\approx}25\%$ for small-area cell $(0.085~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ ) and $\approx20\%$ for mini-module $(12.125~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ , which are much higher than those based on Alfa $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs regardless of the extrinsic Cl-doping condition. Hence, this work has proposed a type of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs with more potential than the current conventional commercial $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NCs in realizing higher performance of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NC-based PSCs in both small and larger scales.
## 4. Experimental Section
Extrinsic $C l{-}S n O_{2}$ NCs: The commercial Alfa $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ NC aqueous dispersion was diluted ten times with ultrapure water. $3.2~\mathsf{m g}$ of MACl was dissolved in $7m L$ of the diluted $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ NC dispersion and vigorously stirred at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for an hour to extrinsically dope the $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ NC with Cl.
Intrinsic $C l{-}S n O_{2}$ NCs: $720~\upmu\upiota$ of $\mathsf{S n C l}_{4}$ was slowly dripped into $6m L$ of mixed solvent containing water and ethanol with the volume ratio of 1:1 in ice bath. The role of $\mathsf{S n C l}_{4}$ is to provide Sn and Cl precursors. The role of ethanol is as a solvent. The role of water is not only as a solvent to mix with ethanol, but also as the $\mathsf{O}$ precursor to react with $\mathsf{S n C l}_{4}$ to obtained intrinsic $C l{-}S n O_{2}$ . The as-prepared solution was stirred vigorously until clear and colorless. Then, $6m L$ of aqueous tetramethylammonium hydroxide solution with a mass fraction of $8\%$ was added drop-by-drop into the reaction system, in order to control the crystal size of the final obtained intrinsic $\mathsf{C l}.\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ NCs as needed.[24] Vigorous stirring is required to re-dissolve the produced white gel into liquid phase. The obtained solution was transferred to autoclave at $200^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for about $\mathsf{\Delta}\mathsf{10h}$ , and it needs to make sure that no precipitation falls out of the solution. Acetone as the antisolvent was then added to precipitate out the as-prepared $\mathsf{C l}.\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ NCs. The obtained $\mathsf{C l}.\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ solid was redispersed to water, and precipitated out again by acetone. This process needs to be repeated several times until no more $\mathsf{C l}.\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ NC is precipitated out from the solution by acetone, to ensure that unwanted impurities are completely removed. Finally, solvent was removed by rotary evaporation, and the remaining $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ NC solids were dried by vacuum.
$F A P b I_{3}$ PSCs: Clean FTO was deposited with $\mathsf{c}_{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ using ALD method. Alfa $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ NC aqueous dispersion that is diluted by a factor of 10, as-prepared extrinsic $\mathsf{C l}.\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ NC aqueous dispersion, and $10\ \mathrm{mg\mL^{-1}}$ of intrinsic $\mathsf{C l}.\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ NC aqueous dispersion were adopted to fabricate the different types of $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ ETLs in this work by spin-coating these dispersions on the $\mathsf{F T O}/\mathsf{c}/\mathsf{\bar{I}i O}_{2}$ substrate at $3000~\mathsf{r p m}$ for $30\ \mathsf{s}_{\mathrm{:}}$ , and then annealed at $180^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ precursor solution composed of $1.5\mathrm{~m~}$ of $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ , $1.5\ \mathsf{m}$ of FAI and $0.53~\mathsf{m}$ of MACl in dimethyl formamide/dimethylsulfoxide mixed-solvent with the volume ratio of 9:1, was deposited on the asprepared $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ NC ETL by spin-coating at 5000 rpm for 30 s. $600~\upmu\up L$ of diethyl ether was rapidly dropped on the film on the countdown of $20~\mathsf{s}$ , and then the film was annealed at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $10\min$ to achieve $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ polycrystalline film. $3~{\mathsf{m g}}~{\mathsf{m L}}^{-1}$ of octylamine hydroiodate in isopropanol was dropped on the as-prepared $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ polycrystalline film, spin-coated at 5000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ and annealed at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\mathrm{~\circ~}}\mathsf{C}$ for 1 min. $72.3\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ of spiro-OMeTAD in chlorobenzene, which had been mixed with $28.8~\upmu\up L$ $\mathsf{m L}^{-1}$ of 4-tert-butylpyridine and $17.5~{\upmu\mathsf{L}}~{\mathsf{m L}}^{-1}$ of Li-TFSI solution in acetonitrile $(520\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}.$ ), was spin-coated on the modified $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ film with a rotating speed of 4000 rpm for $30\mathsf{s}$ . Finally, $100\mathsf{n m}$ of Au electrodes were deposited on the spiro-OMeTAD layer by thermal evaporation.
